# Task 2 – Automating Image Creation with a Dockerfile

## Objective

Automate the creation of the customised Nginx image using a Dockerfile instead of manually editing a running container.

---

## Project Structure

```
tech610-mod-nginx-dockerfile/
│
├── Dockerfile
└── index.html
```

---

## Step 1 – Create the Custom Homepage

A new homepage was created.

Filename:

```
index.html
```

Contents:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>

<head>
    <title>Homaira's Automated Nginx Page</title>
</head>

<body>

<h1>Welcome to Homaira's Automated Nginx Page!</h1>

<p>This page was created automatically using a Dockerfile.</p>

</body>

</html>
```

---

## Step 2 – Create the Dockerfile

A file named exactly:

```
Dockerfile
```

was created containing:

```dockerfile
FROM nginx:latest

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
```

---

## Dockerfile Breakdown

### FROM

```dockerfile
FROM nginx:latest
```

This tells Docker to use the official Nginx image from Docker Hub as the base image.

---

### COPY

```dockerfile
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
```

This automatically replaces the default Nginx homepage with the custom webpage during the build process.

Unlike Task 1, no manual editing of the container is required.

---

## Step 3 – Build the Image

The image was built using:

```bash
docker build -t tech610-nginx-auto:1.0 .
```

### Explanation

| Option | Purpose |
|---------|----------|
| docker build | Builds a Docker image. |
| -t | Assigns the image name and tag. |
| tech610-nginx-auto:1.0 | Image name and version. |
| . | Uses the current directory as the build context. |

During the build Docker automatically:

1. Reads the Dockerfile.
2. Downloads the Nginx image if necessary.
3. Creates a temporary build container.
4. Copies the custom webpage.
5. Creates the new Docker image.
6. Removes the temporary build container.

No manual interaction with a running container is required.

---

## Step 4 – Test the Image

The image was started using:

```bash
docker run -d -p 80:80 --name nginx-auto tech610-nginx-auto:1.0
```

Opening:

```
http://localhost
```

confirmed that the customised homepage was being served.

---

## Step 5 – Tag the Image

Before uploading to Docker Hub the image was tagged using Docker Hub naming conventions.

```bash
docker tag tech610-nginx-auto:1.0 hdaum123/tech610-nginx-auto:1.0
```

Docker Hub images follow the format:

```
dockerhub-username/repository-name:tag
```

---

## Step 6 – Push to Docker Hub

The image was uploaded.

```bash
docker push hdaum123/tech610-nginx-auto:1.0
```

The image can now be downloaded by anyone.

---

## Running the Published Image

```bash
docker run -d -p 80:80 hdaum123/tech610-nginx-auto:1.0
```

or

```bash
docker run -d -p 90:80 hdaum123/tech610-nginx-auto:1.0
```

---

# Why is the Dockerfile Automated?

Task 1 required manually entering the running container.

```
docker exec
↓

Edit index.html
↓

docker commit
```

Task 2 removes these manual steps.

```
Dockerfile
        ↓
docker build
        ↓
Docker follows each instruction automatically
        ↓
Copies index.html
        ↓
Creates the finished image
```

Every build produces exactly the same result.

This makes deployments:

- repeatable
- reliable
- consistent
- less prone to human error

---

# Manual vs Automated

| Manual (`docker commit`) | Automated (`Dockerfile`) |
|---------------------------|--------------------------|
| Modify a running container | Define everything in code |
| Requires manual changes | Fully automated |
| Harder to reproduce | Easily reproducible |
| Good for experimentation | Best practice for production |
| Can introduce inconsistencies | Produces consistent builds |

